---
source: tests/help_snapshots.rs
---
Show the compute hours that runs used.

A compute hour is one core running for one hour. Compute hours are the unit that
Antithesis bills usage in, and that the usage cap counts. They are not
wall-clock hours: Antithesis runs a test in parallel, so one hour of testing
uses many compute hours, and a higher performance tier uses more.

Each run has its ID, the start and end of its billable usage, and its compute
hours.

Columns: RUN ID, STARTED, ENDED, COMPUTE HOURS. COMPUTE HOURS has one decimal
place.

With --rollup, each row is the total of one day, week, or month: its first and
last days, its number of runs, and its compute hours. A run counts in the period
in which it ended. A week starts on Monday. Columns: START, END, RUNS, COMPUTE
HOURS. A period with no runs shows 0.

Times and periods are in the local timezone.

With RUN_IDS, the output has one row for each run, in the order that you give
them. A run has no usage until it ends, so a run that is in progress has no row.
When a run has no usage, the command prints the rows of the other runs, then
fails and names the run.

--after and --before select runs by the time that they ended: --after includes a
run that ended at that time, and --before does not. --before defaults to now.
--after defaults to 1 week before --before. With --rollup, it defaults to 4
weeks before for weeks, and 6 months before for months.

Each takes one of these forms:
  a relative time    `now`, `5 hours ago`, `3 days ago`, `2 weeks ago`
  a date             `2026-10-01`, which is the start of that day
  a time             `2026-10-01 12:00:00`
  an RFC 3339 time   `2026-10-01T12:00:00Z`
A relative time counts minutes, hours, days, weeks or months. A date or a time
without a zone is in the local timezone.

The rows are in the order in which the runs ended, oldest first. With --rollup,
the oldest period is first.

With --rollup, a period is in the output when it holds a selected time. When
--after or --before cuts a period, the total of that period includes only the
runs inside the filter. Without --after, the first period is whole.

Examples:
  snouty runs usage
  snouty runs usage --after "3 days ago"
  snouty runs usage --rollup week
  snouty runs usage --rollup month --after 2026-01-01
  snouty runs usage --after 2026-10-01 --before 2026-11-01
  snouty runs usage <run_id> <run_id>

With --json, each row prints as one JSON object on its own line. The hours field
holds compute hours. A run has the fields run_id, hours, start_time, and
end_time; the times are RFC 3339 in UTC. A period has the fields date, runs, and
hours: date is the last day of the period in the local timezone, and hours is
rounded to hundredths.

Usage: snouty runs usage [OPTIONS] [RUN_IDS]...

Arguments:
  [RUN_IDS]...
          Show only these runs, in this order

Options:
      --rollup <ROLLUP>
          Total the runs of each day, week, or month
          
          [possible values: day, week, month]

      --after <AFTER>
          Only show runs that ended at or after this time: a relative time (e.g.
          3 days ago), a date (e.g. 2026-10-01), a time (e.g. 2026-10-01
          12:00:00) or an RFC 3339 time [default: 1 week before the before time,
          or 4 weeks with a weekly rollup, or 6 months with a monthly rollup]

      --before <BEFORE>
          Only show runs that ended before this time, in the same forms
          [default: now]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --json
          Print NDJSON: one JSON object per line

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
